@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Circuits: breadboard, schematic and PCB (@@EECad))

@box(w=100%, style=offline)
Pressing Start compiles the @@sketch on the server, so the Arduino parts need idoc.page, the local server or the desktop app.
@end

Every block on this page is live in the preview. Each feature gets one line and one working example; the block is also what prints in the PDF.

@section(Breadboard and Arduino circuits: EECad sourdough)

Build a real circuit by hand on a breadboard with an Arduino and real components, then press Start and watch it run: the sketch is compiled and executed on a simulated microcontroller.

@EECad(sourdough)
uno u1 at 40,120
resistor r1 at 320,60 value 220
led d1 at 420,60 color red
wire u1.13 to r1.1 color green
wire r1.2 to d1.a
wire d1.k to u1.gnd color black
@sketch
void setup() { pinMode(13, OUTPUT); }
void loop() { digitalWrite(13, HIGH); delay(500); digitalWrite(13, LOW); delay(500); }
@end
@end

@b{Parts, one per line.} Each line is a part with an id and a place: uno, resistor, led, pushbutton, battery and the rest of the parts drawer.

@EECad(sourdough)
battery b1 at 40,80
resistor r1 at 200,60 value 1k
led d1 at 320,60 color green
wire b1.+ to r1.1
wire r1.2 to d1.a
wire d1.k to b1.-
@end

@b{The program.} An @@sketch section holds real Arduino C++.

@EECad(sourdough, board=uno)
uno u1 at 40,120
@sketch
void setup() { Serial.begin(9600); Serial.println("hello"); }
void loop() {}
@end
@end

@b{A restricted parts drawer.} Name parts after the type and the drawer offers only those, so a lab about LEDs is not lost among 80 parts.

@EECad(sourdough, led, resistor, battery)
@end

@b{Views.} views= picks which views a reader can switch between: breadboard, schematic (drawn from the breadboard's connections) and pcb (routed and DRC-checked).

@EECad(sourdough, views=breadboard;schematic)
battery b1 at 40,80
led d1 at 200,60
wire b1.+ to d1.a
wire d1.k to b1.-
@end

@b{Look of the canvas.} labels=yes shows part names, pinlabels=no hides a board's lettering, grid=no removes the dot grid and bg= colours the surface.

@EECad(sourdough, labels=yes, grid=no, bg=#f7f7f2)
led d1 at 120,60
@end

@b{A graded exercise.} Give id=, pts= and expect= pairs.

@EECad(sourdough, id=lab1, pts=5, expect="count(resistor)=1, led(d1)=on")
battery b1 at 40,80
led d1 at 300,60
@solution
battery b1 at 40,80
resistor r1 at 160,60 value 330
led d1 at 300,60
wire b1.+ to r1.1
wire r1.2 to d1.a
wire d1.k to b1.-
@end

@b{Graded.} With id=, pts= and expect= the server rebuilds the student's circuit from their text, simulates it for run= milliseconds with seed= fixing every noise source, and checks each pair: nets=solution, parts=, count(), V(), current(), blink(), led(), serial~=, burnt=none, net() and apart().

@section(Circuit schematics and simulation: EECad schematic)

Draw a circuit diagram from symbols and wires, check it, and simulate it with ngspice: operating point, transient, AC sweep and DC sweep, plotted in the card.

@EECad(schematic)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,45
R2 resistor 2k at 30,25
GND ground at 30,5
wire 10,40 to 30,40
wire 30,30 to 30,40
@end

@b{Parts and wires.} Each part line is a reference, a kind, a value and a place: resistor, cap, cappol, inductor, sw, vsource, isource, diodes, transistors, op-amps, ground.

@EECad(schematic)
V1 vsource 1V at 0,0
R1 resistor 1k at 20,0
C1 cap 1u at 40,0
GND ground at 60,0
@end

@b{Analyses.} Add a SPICE line: .op, .tran, .ac or .dc.

@EECad(schematic)
V1 vsource 1V at 0,0
R1 resistor 1k at 20,0
C1 cap 1u at 40,0
GND ground at 60,0
.ac dec 30 10 100k
@end

@b{Probes.} probes= lists the nets Simulate plots.

@EECad(schematic, probes=OUT)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,45
GND ground at 30,5
@end

@b{A graded exercise.} expect= pairs read node voltages from .op (V(OUT)=3.33 — label the net you ask about) and the circuit's structure: parts=, count(), erc=clean, missing=, extra=, and nets=solution against the @@solution section.

@EECad(schematic, id=div1, pts=4, expect="count(resistor)=2, erc=clean", tolerance=0.05)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
GND ground at 30,5
@end

@b{Hide the source.} noedit shows the diagram and its tools without the text pane.

@EECad(schematic, noedit)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,45
GND ground at 30,5
@end

@b{Graded.} With id=, pts= and expect= the server rebuilds the student's schematic and checks each pair: voltages from the .op solution within tolerance=, and the structure (parts, counts, ERC, nets=solution compared up to names and placement).

@section(Printed circuit boards: EECad pcb)

Lay out a printed circuit board — parts, tracks, vias, zones — check it against design rules and export Gerbers, or show an existing EAGLE or KiCad board and ask students to find a part on it.

@EECad(pcb)
board 40x30
rules clearance=0.2 track=0.2
net VIN GND OUT
R1 R0805 10k at 10,10 pads 1=VIN 2=OUT
R2 R0805 22k at 10,16 rot 90 pads 1=OUT 2=GND
J1 HDR1x3 at 30,8 pads 1=VIN 2=OUT 3=GND
@end

@b{The board as lines.} board WxH, rules, net names, then each part as a reference, a footprint, a value, a place and its pads' nets; track NET LAYER points, via NET at x,y, zone, text and silkscreen lines.

@EECad(pcb)
board 20x20
net A B
R1 R0805 1k at 6,10 pads 1=A 2=B
track A F.Cu 5,10 2,10
via B at 15,10
@end

@b{View an existing board.} file= shows an EAGLE .brd, a KiCad .kicad_pcb or a .bpcb as it is, with pan and zoom.

@EECad(pcb, file=circuits.assets/board.brd, labels=false)

@b{Find the part.} ask=identify with target= makes a .brd board a question: the student clicks the named part.

@EECad(pcb, file=circuits.assets/board.brd, ask=identify, target=U2)

@b{A graded layout.} expect= pairs check the student's board: drc=clean, unrouted=0, connected=solution (same pads joined as the @@solution key, any route), parts=, count(), missing=, extra=.

@EECad(pcb, id=pcb1, pts=6, expect="drc=clean, unrouted=0")
board 20x20
net A B
R1 R0805 1k at 6,10 pads 1=A 2=B
@end

@b{Graded.} ask=identify is right when the student clicks the target part.


@section(Every other argument)

@b{Simulation controls.} run= is how many milliseconds a graded run lasts, seed= fixes the noise, speed= is simulated seconds per real second, sim=on starts at once, tolerance= is the fraction a reading may be off.

@EECad(sourdough, run=2000, seed=7, speed=0.5, sim=on, tolerance=0.1)
battery b1 at 40,80 value 9V
resistor r1 at 200,60 value 470
led d1 at 320,60 color yellow
wire b1.+ to r1.1
wire r1.2 to d1.a
wire d1.k to b1.-
@end

@b{Drawer, code panel and look.} parts= lists the drawer, code= picks blocks, both, text or none, pinlabels=no hides a board's lettering, stage=theme takes the document's colours, width= and height= size the card, time= is how long a live class keeps it open.

@EECad(sourdough, parts="led resistor pushbutton", code=both, pinlabels=no, stage=theme, width=720, height=420, time=5m)
uno u1 at 40,120
@sketch
void setup() { pinMode(13, OUTPUT); }
void loop() { digitalWrite(13, HIGH); }
@end
@end

@b{Read only.} noedit shows the circuit with no tools; hex= carries a sketch already compiled by a publication, so you never write it by hand.

@EECad(sourdough, noedit)
battery b1 at 40,80
led d1 at 200,60
wire b1.+ to d1.a
wire d1.k to b1.-
@end

@b{Schematic layout and hand-in.} output= puts the result beside the source, submit=server keeps the student's work, height= is the drawing's own height, width= the card's, theme= the studio look.

@EECad(schematic, output=right, submit=server, id=divider-hand-in, height=300, width=700, theme=dark, time=10m)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,45
R2 resistor 2k at 30,25
GND ground at 30,5
wire 10,40 to 30,40
wire 30,30 to 30,40
wire 30,20 to 30,10
@end

@b{Board viewer options.} ratsnest=false hides the unrouted lines, labels=false the designators; noedit, stage=, output=, theme=, time=, width= and height= work as above.

@EECad(pcb, file=circuits.assets/board.brd, ratsnest=false, labels=false, noedit, stage=theme, output=right, theme=dark, time=5m, width=640, height=400)
